{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB08799 - MONDO_grouped_2309_2308",
  "verbalization": "Antazoline is implicated in the pathogenesis of Papillary Conjunctivitis through a series of indirect pathways involving atopic conjunctivitis. The evidence suggests that Antazoline may act as a precursor in the treatment of atopic conjunctivitis, which in turn leads to the development of papillary conjunctivitis. This is supported by the shared indication chain from Antazoline to atopic conjunctivitis, and from there to papillary conjunctivitis in multiple paths. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a plausible, though indirect, relationship between Antazoline and the condition. The mechanism appears to involve the use of Antazoline in the management of atopic conjunctivitis, which may trigger or exacerbate papillary conjunctivitis through underlying inflammatory or allergic processes. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Antazoline to be repurposed for treating papillary conjunctivitis, based on its established role in atopic conjunctivitis.",
